{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789032519348,"sessionID":"ses_f755b0823ffeqOHS10F8XEOvrV","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_d12833004aa2438f83c66545","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls .github/workflows/ 2>/dev/null; ls .gitlab-ci.yml 2>/dev/null; rg -l \"AGENT_CLI_LIVE|agent-cli-contract|agent_cli_contract\" --max-count 1 -g '!src/planning-workflow/tests/**' 2>/dev/null | head -20","description":"Check for CI config and other contract references"},"output":"Makefile\nCLAUDE.md\nskills/status-report/references/agent-conventions.md\nskills/request-council-review/references/headless-delegation.md\nskills/delegate/references/agent-conventions.md\nplugins/planning-workflow/skills/delegate/references/agent-conventions.md\nskills/delegate-task/references/agent-conventions.md\nAGENTS.md\ndocs/agent-cli-contract-tests.md\nskills/consult-the-council/references/members/claude.md\nskills/consult-the-council/scripts/council.py\nplugins/planning-workflow/skills/status-report/references/agent-conventions.md\nplugins/planning-workflow/skills/request-council-review/references/headless-delegation.md\nsrc/planning-workflow/refs/agent-conventions.md\nsrc/planning-workflow/refs/headless-delegation.md\nplugins/planning-workflow/skills/delegate-task/references/agent-conventions.md\nplugins/consult-the-council/skills/consult-the-council/references/members/claude.md\nplugins/consult-the-council/skills/consult-the-council/scripts/council.py\nsrc/consult-the-council/scripts/council.py\nsrc/consult-the-council/tests/test_council.py\n","metadata":{"output":"Makefile\nCLAUDE.md\nskills/status-report/references/agent-conventions.md\nskills/request-council-review/references/headless-delegation.md\nskills/delegate/references/agent-conventions.md\nplugins/planning-workflow/skills/delegate/references/agent-conventions.md\nskills/delegate-task/references/agent-conventions.md\nAGENTS.md\ndocs/agent-cli-contract-tests.md\nskills/consult-the-council/references/members/claude.md\nskills/consult-the-council/scripts/council.py\nplugins/planning-workflow/skills/status-report/references/agent-conventions.md\nplugins/planning-workflow/skills/request-council-review/references/headless-delegation.md\nsrc/planning-workflow/refs/agent-conventions.md\nsrc/planning-workflow/refs/headless-delegation.md\nplugins/planning-workflow/skills/delegate-task/references/agent-conventions.md\nplugins/consult-the-council/skills/consult-the-council/references/members/claude.md\nplugins/consult-the-council/skills/consult-the-council/scripts/council.py\nsrc/consult-the-council/scripts/council.py\nsrc/consult-the-council/tests/test_council.py\n","exit":0,"description":"Check for CI config and other contract references","truncated":false},"title":"Check for CI config and other contract references","time":{"start":1789032519333,"end":1789032519344}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a full picture of the existing system. The user is asking a consultative question:\n\n\"How would you approach keeping instructions that depend on fast-changing CLI flags from going stale, and what would you want to know first?\"\n\nThis is essentially asking me to evaluate the existing approach and think about what more could be done. The existing system has:\n\n1. **Layer 0**: CLI installed & version >= verified_version\n2. **Layer 1**: --help advertises every contracted flag and enum value (drift detector)\n3. **Layer 2**: sources use only contracted flags; contract flags are used somewhere (bidirectional)\n4. **Layer 2b**: evals harness argv matches contract\n5. **Layer 3**: opt-in live smoke test (bills tokens)\n\nThe question is what's missing / how to keep it from going stale. Key gaps I can identify:\n\n1. **When does the drift get detected?** The current system only detects drift when someone runs the pytest suite *after* a CLI upgrade. If CLIs release weekly, the suite is green on Monday and the CLI breaks the skills on Tuesday. Nothing *watches* for the change. The detection is pull-based (run the tests) rather than push-based (scheduled CI, dependabot-style version watching).\n\n2. **--help lying**: A flag can be advertised in --help but behave differently (e.g., semantics changed, flag accepted but ignored, deprecation warnings). The help contract catches renames/removals but not semantic drift. The live smoke covers one canonical invocation per agent, but only for one command path, and it's opt-in (bills tokens).\n\n3. **Version matrix**: The tests run against *whatever is installed locally*. There's no record of \"which CLI versions the flags were verified against\" beyond a single `verified_version`. If a flag works in 2.1.263 and breaks in 2.2.0, and you need to support a range, there's no matrix.\n\n4. **CI cadence**: Is the suite run on a schedule with freshly-updated CLIs? Weekly CLI releases + manual test runs = staleness window of up to a week+.\n\n5. **Failure → fix loop**: When test 1 fails, the instructions say \"fix the skill refs AND this file\" — but the compiled skills (./skills/ at repo root, plugins/) also need recompiling. Is there anything ensuring the compiled output gets regenerated? Actually the compile step presumably runs in CI.\n\n6. **Coverage of the scanner**: scan_sources finds invocations in .md/.sh/.py/.toml/.yaml. If someone writes a flag in a way the regex misses (e.g., built dynamically, f-string, variable indirection), the scanner won't catch it. There's a guard test (test_scanner_finds_the_known_invocations) but only for a handful of known invocations. Dynamic argv construction (like evals' build_argv) is handled specially.\n\n7. **What about flags that exist but are deprecated/renamed with a shim?** Many CLIs keep old flags working but print deprecation warnings. --help might stop advertising them before they're removed, or continue advertising after they're no-ops.\n\nSo \"what would I want to know first?\" — clarifying questions:\n\n1. Where does the suite run and how often? (local dev only? CI on schedule? on CLI upgrade?) The staleness question is mostly a *detection latency* question: what's the max acceptable window between a CLI release breaking a flag and us knowing?\n\n2. Who consumes the skills, and can they be hurt before we detect the drift? (e.g., background workers stalling on permission prompts — mentioned in the docstring). Is a fail-fast at runtime needed (e.g., a preflight check in the skills themselves)?\n\n3. Is there a version support policy — must skills work across a range of CLI versions (e.g., consumers with older installs), or only the latest?\n\n4. Are the CLIs upgraded automatically (brew autoupdate, npm etc.) on dev machines/CI runners, or manually? Do we control the environment?\n\n5. Budget for live smoke tests (bills tokens) — can we afford nightly live smokes per agent?\n\n6. Are semantic changes in scope (flag exists but behaves differently), or only existence/renames?\n\nThe question says \"How would you approach...\" — so they want my approach + clarifying questions. This is plan mode, and per the CLAUDE.md instructions: \"When you need user input, break it down into single numbered items... Ask, wait for answers, then continue.\"\n\nI should present:\n1. My assessment of what the current system already covers (brief)\n2. The gaps / my approach to fill them\n3. Numbered clarifying questions\n\nLet me think about the approach more carefully.\n\n**The core problem framing**: instructions (skill sources) hardcode flags; flags churn weekly. Existing mitigation: a contract (TOML) + 4-layer test suite. The question is how to keep *that* from going stale — i.e., the meta-problem: detection latency, coverage blind spots, and semantic drift.\n\n**My approach** would layer these:\n\nA. **Reduce the detection latency to ~zero relative to CLI releases**: scheduled (e.g., daily/nightly) CI job that (1) installs/upgrades all CLIs to latest, (2) runs the cheap layers (0, 1, 2, 2b — no tokens), and optionally the live smoke. This converts \"we find out when someone next runs tests locally\" into \"we find out within 24h of the release.\" Pin the exact CLI versions in CI (or record them) so a failure is reproducible.\n\nB. **Push-based early warning**: watch the CLIs' release notes/changelogs/repos (RSS/GitHub releases API) — a scheduled job that flags \"new release N available; contract verified against N-1\" and opens an issue to re-verify. Also `--version` check could compare against latest known release.\n\nC. **Runtime preflight / fail fast**: since the failure mode is nasty (headless worker stalls on a prompt nobody answers — from the docstring), add a cheap preflight in the skills themselves: before launching a worker, run `<cmd> --help` (or a cached result) and grep for the critical flags; refuse early with a clear message instead of hanging. This bounds the blast radius when drift happens between CI runs. Maybe this is overkill — but worth asking. Actually the herdr timeout + wait-output --match might already handle some of it, but a permission prompt isn't the same as a hang... Actually herdr agent prompt --wait --timeout exists, so stalls are bounded. Still, the failure is silent-ish.\n\nD. **Semantic drift**: --help existence checks can't catch behavior changes. Mitigations: the live smoke covers the canonical invocation (opt-in). Could extend to a \"canary matrix\": for each distinct *behavioral pattern* (permission bypass, read-only mode, headless output capture), one minimal live invocation, run on a schedule, asserting on observable behavior (exits 0, expected stdout). Token cost is small if prompts are \"reply with exactly: PONG\". But needs auth + budget.\n\nE. **Coverage of the scanner itself**: periodic audit — run scanner inventory, diff against contract; the guard test exists but could be extended: assert every skill that shells out to an agent has at least one scanned invocation (catch \"new skill added with invocation patterns the regex misses\"). Also handle dynamically constructed flags (the build_argv case) — maybe require those modules to import the contract or be listed in an exclusion registry.\n\nF. **Version floor policy in the contract is good** — verified_version + \"installed must be >= verified\". Keep that. Consider adding a `verified_at`/`latest_known_release` and a soft-warning when the installed version is much newer than verified (drift window).\n\nActually wait — the current test only fails if installed is *older* than verified. If the CLI is much *newer*, tests still check --help, so they'd catch removals at that point. That's fine when tests run. The staleness issue is cadence, not direction.\n\nG. **Documentation/knowledge**: each flag in the contract has a comment saying which skill uses it and why — already partially there (comments). Keep the \"why\" so when a flag is renamed, an agent can find the replacement semantic (e.g., \"bypassPermissions\" → new mode name) without spelunking.\n\nNow — \"what would you want to know first?\" — the numbered questions. Per CLAUDE.md I must present each question as a separate numbered item, max helpful. Questions:\n\n1. Where/how often does the contract suite run today — local only, or also on a schedule in CI? And do CI runners auto-upgrade the CLIs? (determines detection latency)\n2. What's the acceptable detection window / blast radius? Are there consumers outside this repo who get hurt by a stale flag before we catch it?\n3. Is the token budget for scheduled live smokes (per agent per run) acceptable? Any auth constraints in CI (headless auth)?\n4. Do we need to support a *range* of CLI versions (consumers with older installs), or is \"latest installed\" the only target?\n5. Is semantic/behavioral drift in scope, or just flag existence/renames?\n\nMaybe 4-6 questions max. Keep it tight.\n\nAlso should I mention what's already good? Briefly — the existing design is solid (bidirectional contract, version floor, scanner, live smoke). The gaps: detection is pull-based, coverage of the scanner is regex-heuristic, semantic drift invisible to --help.\n\nThe response format: plan mode, read-only, I've done my research. I should present a concise assessment + approach + numbered questions. The user asked a conversational design question — they want my thinking, not a file. So the answer should be prose (structured), concise but this warrants more than 4 lines since they asked \"how would you approach... and what would you want to know first\" — a design consultation. The system prompt says fewer than 4 lines unless user asks for detail; this question explicitly asks for approach + questions, so moderate length is justified. But keep it tight — no fluff.\n\nStructure:\n\n**What you already have** (2-3 lines)\n\n**Where staleness still leaks in** (the gaps) — this is the core answer:\n\n1. Detection latency — tests run when someone runs them; CLI releases weekly → up to a week+ of silent breakage. Fix: scheduled CI with fresh CLI installs.\n2. Pull vs push — nothing watches releases. Fix: release-notes watcher that opens an issue.\n3. --help can't catch semantic drift — flag exists, behavior changed; only live smoke covers it, and it's opt-in. Fix: scheduled canary smokes.\n4. Scanner is heuristic — new skills with novel invocation styles slip through; guard test covers 7 known cases only. Fix: per-skill coverage assertion.\n5. Runtime blast radius — workers stall on permission prompts. Fix: preflight check in skills (cheap --help grep) before launching headless workers.\n\n**Questions** (numbered, per CLAUDE.md).\n\nLet me write this concisely. I'll aim for a compact but complete answer. No file edits — plan mode. I might not need more exploration; I've read the contract, the machinery, and the tests. Maybe quickly check if there's CI config in the repo to answer my own question #1 partially. Let me check for .github/workflows.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_08aa5c847001Q