{"type":"text","timestamp":1789032539739,"sessionID":"ses_f755b0823ffeqOHS10F8XEOvrV","part":{"id":"prt_08aa5df96001eo4EwHG721PA3d","messageID":"msg_08aa5d734002yfx3Sd41cmt8wf","sessionID":"ses_f755b0823ffeqOHS10F8XEOvrV","type":"text","text":"I've read the contract TOML, the machinery (`agent_cli_contract.py`), and the pytest suite. The existing design is sound — the staleness question is no longer \"how do we detect drift\" (layers 0–3 do that) but \"**when** does detection run\" and \"**what can't --help see**\". Here's my read:\n\n## Where staleness still leaks in\n\n1. **Detection latency is unbounded.** There's no scheduled CI (no `.github/workflows`), so the suite runs only when a human runs it, against whatever CLI happens to be installed. A weekly-release CLI can break a flag the day after a green run and the repo stays silently broken for days — with the nasty failure mode you already documented: headless workers stalling on permission prompts nobody can answer. Fix: a nightly scheduled CI job that upgrades all six CLIs to latest, records exact versions, and runs the cheap layers (0/1/2/2b — no tokens), plus optionally the live smoke. This collapses the window from \"whenever someone next runs pytest\" to ≤24h after a release.\n\n2. **Detection is pull-based.** Nothing *watches* the CLIs. A tiny scheduled job polling GitHub releases/changelogs for the six CLIs could open an issue the moment a release lands (\"codex 0.152.0 out; contract verified against 0.151.0 — re-verify\"), turning a passive failure into an active work item before anyone hits it.\n\n3. **--help can't catch semantic drift.** A flag can stay advertised while its behavior changes (deprecation shim, ignored-with-warning, changed enum meaning). The live smoke is the only behavioral check, it covers one canonical invocation per agent, and it's opt-in. Fix: a small canary matrix — one minimal live invocation per *behavioral pattern* (permission bypass, read-only reviewer, headless output capture), run on schedule. \"Reply with exactly: PONG\" prompts cost almost nothing.\n\n4. **The scanner is heuristic and its coverage isn't asserted per-skill.** New skills with invocation styles the regex misses (dynamically built argv, f-strings) slip through silently; `test_scanner_finds_the_known_invocations` pins only 7 cases, and the `build_argv` case needed a special-cased test. Fix: a coverage assertion — every skill that mentions an agent CLI name must yield ≥1 scanned invocation, forcing new/dynamic call sites to register explicitly.\n\n5. **Blast radius between runs is unguarded.** When drift does slip in, the skill discovers it by hanging. Fix: a cheap preflight in the skills themselves — before launching an unattended worker, grep `<cmd> --help` for the 2–3 critical flags and fail fast with \"your claude 2.2.0 no longer supports X, update the skill\" instead of a silent stall.\n\n## What I'd want to know first\n\n1. Where is the suite expected to run — is there any CI today (I found none in-repo), and do you control the environment where CLIs get upgraded, or do contributors' machines drift independently?\n2. What's the acceptable detection window and who gets hurt in the meantime — is this repo the only consumer, or do downstream installs (marketplace/plugin consumers) hit stale flags before you do?\n3. Is there token budget + headless auth available for scheduled live canaries, or must behavioral checks stay opt-in?\n4. Must skills work across a *range* of CLI versions (consumers with older installs), or is \"latest release, floor-enforced\" the only target?\n5. Is semantic/behavioral drift in scope for this effort, or strictly flag existence/renames?\n\nAnswer whichever you have opinions on, and I'll turn this into a concrete plan.","time":{"start":1789032521622,"end":1789032539733}}}
